Session Road is one of the busiest streets in Baguio City. It is the main thoroughfare of the city and the main hub of Baguio's Central Business District.

&nbsp;

<strong>History</strong>

In April 22 to June 11, 1904, the Philippine Commission held its sessions in the old Baden-Powell Hall that is located in Session Road and that is how it derived its name. During that time Baguio was officially declared as the Summer Capital of the Philippines. A marker by what is now the Baden-Powell Inn, located beside colossal bus terminals on Governor Pack Road, mounts as the only apparent evidence that a certain historical significance ever happened to the Session Road.

&nbsp;

<strong>About</strong>

Session Road is now the center of most of the routes in Baguio City. Because it is located in a very prime area, it is almost always overcrowded and gridlocked. But regardless of the traffic and crowd, people still flock the area because most of the business establishments are located there. Restaurants, cafés, bazaars, boutiques, old theatres, banks and grocery stores are among the few business establishments located in the area. At the lower part of this road is the Baguio City Public Market and a little hike going up to the opposite direction is the SM City Baguio.

&nbsp;

People have a lot of reason why they go to Session Road. For students and locals, it is a place to gather with friends and peers since there is a regular jam session of reggae, folk, and indigenous Filipino music by the local band called "sessiOnroad". Bars and KTVs are also located in the are making it a perfect place for night lifes. For tourists, this place is a place of discovery, a starting point for adventure. Jeepneys going to the famous Baguio City Tourist Spots are mostly located here. Cafés and restaurants that serve international and even local dishes are also located in the area, making it easier for tourists to taste and experience what Baguio City has to offer. The cold weather that most tourists are longing for is a bonus!

According to wikipedia "<b>Allied Banking Corporation</b>  is now <strong>Philippine National Bank</strong> was one of the largest banks in the Philippines. On February 9, 2013, the bank was merged with Philippine National Bank, creating the fourth largest private domestic bank in the Philippines."

